# Normalise line endings.
#
# The repository is edited on Windows, developed on Linux CI, and built in Linux
# containers. Without this, `git diff` shows whole files as changed when a
# checkout lands on a different platform, which makes a review of a five-line
# change impossible.
#
# `* text=auto` with `eol=lf` gives LF in the repository and LF on checkout
# everywhere. On Windows, Git's `core.autocrlf` then converts on the way in and
# back on the way out, so the working tree stays native without the index ever
# recording a CRLF.
#
# The language-specific binary lines below matter: without them Git will try to
# normalise a PNG and produce a file that is subtly corrupt, and the encoding
# guard in `scripts/check-encoding.js` cannot tell a mangled image from a
# mangled comment.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must stay LF or they fail to run on Linux.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Windows-only, and explicitly CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary. Never normalised.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary

# Lockfiles are generated with LF and diffed in review, so normalise them.
package-lock.json text eol=lf
pnpm-lock.yaml text eol=lf
yarn.lock text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf

# Generated - mark so a diff on them is obviously a build artefact.
*.pb.go linguist-generated=true
client/next-env.d.ts linguist-generated=true
client/.next/** linguist-generated=true
client/.next-env.d.ts linguist-generated=true
